[Music]  Nosferatu is a classic but unofficial  cinematic adaptation of Bram Stoker's  1897 novel Dracula the film released in  1922 is a silent German expressionist  horror film and an absolute Masterpiece  that pioneered new techniques and helped  establish horror tropes that remain in  effect even today more than a hundred  years after it was made  but perhaps its greatest contribution to  horror is its reimagining of the vampire  Dracula as count orlock a monster so  iconic that his very Shadow is  recognizable you could even argue that  orlock has spawned his own monstrous  line of progeny one that runs parallel  to the aristocratic Draculas of stoker  and Lugosi but this horrific animalistic  ugly vampire very different from  lugosi's is not maybe quite as popular  as his line but he can still be seen in  film TV shows video games and other  forms of Pop Culture too  I'm Dr Brittany Warman and I'm Dr Sarah  klito and we're folklorists who study  vampires  [Music]  Nosferatu was produced by pranafilm a  short-lived silent era German Film  Studio founded in 1921 by Enrico Dickman  and artist Albin growl it's important to  remember that Nosferatu is an unlicensed  and unofficial adaptation of Bram  Stoker's Dracula it's very obviously  drawing from the book but because a  bunch of the names and the details are  changed product film claimed that it  wasn't really copyright infringement  yeah and this didn't really go that well  for them there was a huge copyright  battle that happened around this film so  Bram Stoker had died by the time the  film was released but his wife Florence  stoker was not pleased she'd actually  already denied the company the rights to  the film they were like we want to make  this movie she was like no thank you but  they went ahead and made it anyway it's  just pretty gutsy right there pretty  gutsy so the stoker estate sued and it  wasn't a hard case for them to win the  original German version of the film had  enter titles that specifically said  Dracula not the greatest mistake to make  if you're trying to claim you weren't  infringing on copyright yeah and the  plot was obviously incredibly similar  they didn't really have a leg to stand  on so after the stoker estate won the  case the judge actually ordered that all  the copies of the film be destroyed so  it's pretty amazing that we even have  this film at all we were this close to  not having Nosferatu which has had such  an enormous impact on you know not only  vampire films but horror films in  general and also just our cultural  conception of what a vampire looks like  and what they act like it's wild  [Music]  so there are a few pretty crucial  differences between the vampire and Bram  Stoker's Dracula and count orlock in  nosferatsu probably the first thing it's  a little obvious is that count orlock is  called orlock instead of Dracula and  again this is largely because of the  copyright issue they were like oh God if  we just straight up call them Dracula  might be in trouble  so  obviously Nosferatu is a silent film so  minimal dialogue and what dialogue there  is is conveyed through writing on  intertile frames  character and ideas have to be conveyed  through body language and facial  expressions rather than the endless  writing generated by all the characters  in Dracula  so Dracula the novel is an epistolary  novel which means that it is compiled  from documents this means it's basically  made up of letters diary entries  sometimes newspaper clippings stuff like  that it's all writing that the  characters themselves have generated  expressing their own points of view like  from their first person perspective so  changing all of this into a format a  silent film where there's almost no  dialogue at all and what dialogue there  is has to show up on the screen written  out is an amazing transformation  most of the action from Nosferatu is  really just like the first third of  Dracula while the second like roughly  two-thirds of the novel are largely  compressed into the final you know like  30 minutes or so of the film  instead there's also much more focus on  the vampire and kind of what he's up to  rather than following a large cast of  characters in Britain  [Music]  oh  Sarah and I are folklorists so one of  the most interesting parts of Nosferatu  to us is that count warlock is much more  like the vampires of folklore than later  cinematic reincarnations of the vampire  he barely passes for human and arguably  he even doesn't pass for human he really  does and he's more like his rats or even  the werewolf which is an amazing part of  this movie they used a striped hyena as  a werewolf in the film and it's pretty  fantastic yeah and we're clearly  supposed to you know equate the vampire  you know with his rats with this  werewolf in the forest that seems to be  you know linked to him in some way and  again this is a lot more like some of  the old folklore we see around vampires  where vampires can't really pass for  human where they're much more  animalistic a lot of the time they're  you know  ugly and gross because they're so  bloated from blood so we're not seeing  that here exactly but we are seeing kind  of a grotesque in humanity that sets him  apart from the people who surround him  even the connection to the werewolf is  really interesting because in folklore  vampires and werewolves are we're not  really the separate categories that we  tend to think give them as today there  was a lot of slippage between the two of  those so we're not sure if orlock is the  werewolf in this movie or if the  werewolves another bad guy hanging out  but the fact that that it exists that  questioning exists seems like an  interesting part of the film too  yeah there's even some old folklore that  suggests that if you are a werewolf in  life you are a vampire in death so  there's this you know long-term link  this possibility pairing these different  kinds of monsters together blurring the  boundaries between them  foreign  [Music]  's performance is what's ultimately the  most mesmerizing thing about it even  today he's got pointed ears hunched  shoulders they stick thin body Talons  extending from the tips of his fingers  his fingers are so long gone to chalk  white face all together it's quite an  impression  also there's just just so many shots of  orloc that are completely iconic now  from his silhouette gliding up the  stairs to his angular Ascent from his  coffin I mean he's just like and like it  feels like that should be funny  it's not no it really isn't and it is  it's still haunting yeah it's still  haunting people you know make fun of it  now and do it as sort of a parody but in  the nosferati film it is legitimately  spooky legitimately spooky and just his  shape in general his shadow it's all  instantly recognizable nowadays  and this is just such a contrast to the  like sort of melodramatic like big  gestures and frenetic activity of the  other actors that surround him and he's  just there silent and still and then  moving very slowly and that contrast  just heightens the difference between  him and the other characters heightens  his own uncanniness and his inhumanity  it really does it creates this  incredible Gothic weirdness  despite its lack of jump scares or showy  set pieces or gory CGI still to use the  famous film critic Roger Ebert's words  is effective it doesn't scare us but it  haunts us  [Music]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  [Music]

